      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5fcd676570b59138e6ef0271|full_name}: I used ETC and crunch. The crunch allows reduction in size of up to 90 % or so. If you enable crunch but keep the quality maximum you will get a reduction in size of around 30% with little to no visual loss. Crunch compression is individualistic per texture so you will want to fine tune the size vs quality per texture. Something that you can't really do with ASTC. In terms of comparing it to 6x6 ASTC I found that it does in fact take up less space than ETC at the same exact resolution. The problem is though, that with ETC you are able to use higher resolutions alongside crunch compression, to reduce the size of the assets which you cannot do with ASTC. For example: you can have a 2048 texture with crunch compression and it would take up less space than a 1024 texture with ASTC 6x6. Now yes the 2048 texture would use more memory at runtime but it would also result in a higher quality image despite the crunch compression and it would also result in a smaller total package overall. If you were comparing the 1024 ASTC with ETC textures ASTC would have the advantage always even at 6x6 compression. My problem is that the assets which used this sort of method before need to be remade due to the fact that ASTC will force 2048x2048 resolution in that example.",
